(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-3-antiarrhythmic-class-3-quiz"]={"title":"Class III: Potassium Channel Blockers Quiz 1 — Pharmacology I Exam 3","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which drug blocks potassium, sodium and calcium channels as well as alpha and beta receptors?","choices":["Amiodarone","Dofetilide","Sotalol","Propafenone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Amiodarone blocks potassium channels, inactivated sodium channels, calcium channels and alpha and beta receptors. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic's level rises when amiodarone is added?","choices":["Adenosine","Esmolol","Flecainide","Magnesium sulfate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Amiodarone increases flecainide and procainamide, as well as digoxin, warfarin and phenytoin. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which restriction applies to prescribing dofetilide?","choices":["Prescribers need special training","It must be given intravenously","It needs loading doses","It is avoided with beta blockers"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Dofetilide has limited availability, restricted to those with special training. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic class prolongs repolarization by blocking potassium channels?","choices":["Class I","Class II","Class III","Class IV"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Class 3 drugs are the potassium channel blockers that prolong repolarization: amiodarone, dofetilide and sotalol. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Why may amiodarone require loading doses?","choices":["Its half-life is very short","It has extensive first-pass metabolism","It is eliminated unchanged by the kidneys","Its half-life is very long"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Amiodarone's half-life averages about 40 days, with slow onset and slow elimination, so loading doses may be needed. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which amiodarone toxicity is listed as potentially fatal?","choices":["Corneal deposits","Photosensitivity","Blue-gray skin","Pulmonary fibrosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pneumonitis and pulmonary fibrosis are amiodarone's potentially fatal toxicity. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which neurologic effects are listed for amiodarone?","choices":["Seizures and nystagmus","Neuropathy and ataxia","Tinnitus and dizziness","Sedation and fatigue"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Amiodarone's neurotoxicity includes neuropathy, muscle weakness and ataxia. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Sotalol combines the actions of which two antiarrhythmic classes?","choices":["Classes I and II","Classes II and III","Classes I and III","Classes II and IV"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Sotalol has beta-blocking (class II) activity and inhibits potassium channels (class III). — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which change in the cardiac action potential is the signature of class III drugs?","choices":["Slower phase 0 upstroke","Delayed phase 3 repolarization","Earlier phase 3 repolarization","Flatter phase 4 depolarization"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Class III drugs prolong phase 3, so the action potential and the refractory period both last longer. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Why can amiodarone cause hyperthyroidism or hypothyroidism?","choices":["It is a thyroid hormone analog","It blocks alpha and beta receptors","It is cleared unchanged by the kidneys","It inhibits the sodium-potassium pump"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Amiodarone is an analog of thyroid hormone, and both hyperthyroidism and hypothyroidism are on its toxicity list. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which pair of toxicities is listed for sotalol?","choices":["Lupus and marrow suppression","Cinchonism and thrombocytopenia","Hypotension and edema","Torsades and bronchospasm"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Sotalol's toxicities are torsades de pointes (from potassium channel block) and bronchospasm (from beta blockade). — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Dofetilide is listed for which use?","choices":["Slowing the ventricular rate","Terminating torsades de pointes","Normal rhythm in atrial fibrillation","Paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Dofetilide's listed indication is normal rhythm in atrial fibrillation. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 34"}]};
